// result_end

In the example, both items have five categories, but responses to x pile up at the top of the scale and responses to y at the bottom. The Pearson correlation of the codes is 0.49 and Spearman's 0.51, both well below the latent 0.6. The polychoric estimates recover it: 0.60 (two-step) and 0.61 (ML), with intervals that cover 0.6. The LR test finds no conflict with bivariate normality (p = 0.64), and the ML thresholds reproduce the generating cut-points to within sampling error. With 25 cells and 400 observations, the two estimators differ by less than a tenth of a standard error.

The simulation shows how strongly coarsening attenuates a correlation. With equal-probability categories, the Pearson correlation of the codes is 0.34 for binary items, 0.41 for three categories and 0.47 for seven. Thresholds skewed in opposite directions make it worse: 0.24 for binary items, less than half the latent value. The polychoric estimate is unbiased in every design, its standard errors match the sampling SD, and Fisher-z intervals cover at 91–97%. The attenuation of the codes is not a fixed factor, because it depends on the marginal distributions. Correlations of differently skewed items cannot be compared, and factor analyses of code correlations recover too many or distorted factors.

Polychoric correlation is not robust to its distributional assumption. Under a latent t₃ dependence, where the margins are normal after transformation but the tails are jointly heavy, the target normal-score correlation is 0.489. The polychoric estimate is biased downward, to 0.40 for binary items and 0.48 for seven categories, and coverage drops to 76–90%. The thresholds absorb any monotone transformation of the margins; the joint shape of the latent distribution is what matters. The LR test detects the misfit in 42–62% of samples once there are three or more categories, but it is blind for 2 × 2 tables (df = 0). It is also only approximately calibrated with sparse tables: 8% with five or seven equal categories, and 1–4% when skewed margins leave many cells nearly empty.

>> Practical guidance

- Use polychoric correlations for ordinal items with few categories (fewer than about seven), especially when the items are skewed differently, and as input to factor analysis of such items, using weighted least squares or diagonally weighted estimators for the model.
- The two-step estimator is as good as full ML in practice, and much faster for large item sets. Use ML when the thresholds and their standard errors are of interest.
- Check bivariate normality with the LR test where df > 0. Treat a rejection as a sign that the "latent correlation" is not well defined, not only that the estimate is noisy.
- Zero cells are allowed, but a 2 × 2 table with a zero cell gives an estimate at the boundary (±1). Report such cases rather than the capped value.
- A matrix of pairwise polychoric correlations need not be positive definite. Smooth it, for example with the nearest correlation matrix, before inverting.

>> References

- K. Pearson (1900). Mathematical contributions to the theory of evolution. VII. On the correlation of characters not quantitatively measurable. *Philosophical Transactions of the Royal Society A* 195, 1-47. doi:10.1098/rsta.1900.0022
- U. Olsson (1979). Maximum likelihood estimation of the polychoric correlation coefficient. *Psychometrika* 44(4), 443-460. doi:10.1007/BF02296207
- A. Genz (2004). Numerical computation of rectangular bivariate and trivariate normal and t probabilities. *Statistics and Computing* 14, 251-260. doi:10.1023/B:STCO.0000035304.20635.31
- J. Fox (2022). polycor: Polychoric and Polyserial Correlations. R package version 0.8.
- F. Holgado-Tello, S. Chacón-Moscoso, I. Barbero-García and E. Vila-Abad (2010). Polychoric versus Pearson correlations in exploratory and confirmatory factor analysis of ordinal variables. *Quality & Quantity* 44, 153-166. doi:10.1007/s11135-008-9190-y
